#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065317} :activity/id #uuid "bdb82b74-3fdc-5083-a106-20c7dc1287f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411 :topic/id #uuid "46d83f8d-b8f7-5858-99d2-3e75f2b9bd8c" :topic/math-academy-id 287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065384 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're told that the radius of a circle is **growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second**, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27d0543834333202411a9700401b7e33329a255c7103c9f2ebe394d8c1cf694b.png)\n\nIf the radius of the circle is growing, then its area is also increasing. How do we calculate the **rate at which the area is increasing**?\n\nThe rate at which the area is changing can be expressed using a derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is the area of the circle, measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2,$ and $t$ is the time in seconds. \n\nWe know that the area is related to the radius $r$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the radius of a circle is growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt} = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting this information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot 2\\pi r=4\\pi r\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd, that's it, we're done! \n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $r$ at that moment into the above. So, for example, if we want to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=6\\,\\textrm{cm},$ we plug this into the above and get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\pi \\cdot 6 = 24\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6925e269-127a-5cf1-8116-aa809d1cf42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2f58c76e-8d14-575a-b5de-62cc2003eb38" :step/math-academy-id 906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064023 :question/id #uuid "7aceecc7-33ca-5a7e-a4aa-cf1a313785fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506590}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "a12978db-32b6-52fc-a426-abfda5e434ae" :step/math-academy-id 907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :question/id #uuid "fba09533-20f9-5e4c-9786-cfbe7b179512" :question/math-academy-id "e-226"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371056}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "9c671f5f-a9e8-5694-8457-ab3f9df8b239" :step/math-academy-id 910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064025 :question/id #uuid "87c5af76-7652-525a-aeb2-d419bab613e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506596}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "318a88e0-e4a9-5b31-a8c3-9cb7712ff53b" :step/math-academy-id 913}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064393}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506663} :activity/id #uuid "2a8712d4-6984-598d-9326-16635a3b14bc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411 :topic/id #uuid "46d83f8d-b8f7-5858-99d2-3e75f2b9bd8c" :topic/math-academy-id 287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f09fc632-c551-511d-b5ff-0a2ef83dfc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fdc4f0-4261-5d00-b2fa-9b5690965424" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9d699d5-e68c-517c-8c66-c0218f7be321" :question/math-academy-id "q-344727" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e09441c0ab651a759862d3689aee517d089a43d2089697a1b77882de1e5d73.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a patio forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{m/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{m}?$\n\nThe area of the pool is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{5}{2\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} :step/id #uuid "04cf9317-baff-533e-bded-bc0975c757e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e5c2fc-62c3-5cff-8c3f-12578ac7eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505922} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd9e9ba-35f5-5dc8-b23d-1b83df5ca67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506037} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad7d797-78b2-528c-9d9b-806dc635caa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506068} {:answer/id #uuid "8c834b34-6910-5d98-ab6c-76660ea17f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506154} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ab0c31-9934-5df4-8de8-4658ed227b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207} :answer-field/id #uuid "893ce6e9-d2a5-5687-890a-43b20d335c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3340fda4-31bb-56af-bac8-5dcbd67440f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116730" :question/problem "A spherical hot air balloon is inflated. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the balloon is numerically equal to nine-halfs the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the balloon at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =18\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(18\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =324\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =324\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =81 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9bc4ee9-11ca-5a48-8b4d-0b8a2d853ce7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f771e8-4826-5b5d-8228-a990eba66454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369205} {:answer/id #uuid "75a1d081-eea5-5784-82dd-15b7ad720ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.5\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369206} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9a3ece-f0fc-5902-8ec9-1de1e79a9224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369207} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7b5843-d568-544c-8d66-0fe759b8fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369208} {:answer/id #uuid "73d4ba9d-d85b-5d24-b9bf-734a0c435e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369206} :answer-field/id #uuid "2882600e-dd6b-59ca-8e2e-a290902aa712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d90b811-f213-55fb-a391-b4b1340be2c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116647" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a864a350b6493794639ea031c7d40242717f5e7325eb326228f82a9cb1c10c9.png)\n\nA graphic designer is animating a circle in such a way that the area of the circle increases at a rate of $7\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is the radius of the circle increasing at the moment when the radius is $1\\text{ cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=1,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\pi $ and $r=1,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (1) \\\\ 7\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $3.5$ centimeters per second."} :step/id #uuid "bdda543f-34f7-5aaf-9190-3fecab0d3532" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7482730a-2ef8-51f5-a35d-2f7208beda4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d1d24c-d1f3-5b29-bd9f-d25502738b67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37194e35-397f-5f0f-b8ce-0e1f1331cb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344726" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c16496b13f9ad9fc995cc59dedf1777f25e394ca61dd2ea493d8bd8f69c5e14.png)\n\nPaint dripping onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The radius of the puddle increases at a rate of $10\\,\\text{in/min}.$ How fast is the area of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{9}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10$ and $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{9}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f7e7553f-61e4-5efb-b004-ea189584c1c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cb41b0-01ea-5c46-86e5-04b8117b3dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505946} {:answer/id #uuid "22fcb82c-2909-591f-be60-e9e88a12eb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505967} {:answer/id #uuid "67a91bd3-65bd-58c2-86c6-7c75df5bee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506087} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1966ac-a96b-5e2d-9d3c-63f6e79e8a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506125} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ad83a5-b560-5894-9b06-c5bb1a83e3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505946} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61e3d80-f1fd-5eef-8661-45e65506ae2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8399078-36ec-5b21-87c0-73ffc0d26600" :question/math-academy-id "q-2550" :question/problem "![](images/97/976db172587f6acdc7af6e426b01adf477e86ef731ce92939e122e291b53337b.png)\n\nA hot air balloon is inflated at the rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is the radius, $r$, changing at the moment when $r=2\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=2\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $r=2,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the balloon is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}$ when the radius is $2\\,\\text{in}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fe01ad7d-1637-5e4b-88cb-7874304a764d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506650}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505904}]}
